{
  "requirement": "`ToolUsage` holds the cumulative usage for one tool.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct ToolUsage {\n    pub calls: u64,\n    pub result_bytes: u64,\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for ToolUsage;\nimpl Copy for ToolUsage;\nimpl Debug for ToolUsage;\nimpl Default for ToolUsage;\nimpl Serialize for ToolUsage;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage` states only a public type declaration and a list of trait implementations. It states no runtime behavior. Counting behavior is delegated to the refining requirements `ToolUsage.calls`, `ToolUsage.result_bytes`, `ToolUsage.by_name` and `ToolUsage.replay`. These are separate requirements with their own tests, so I did not assess them here.\n\nThe requirement is anchored with `:test:` to `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`. That test checks each obligation as follows:\n- **Inventory:** `specified_exports` parses `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md`. It lists `struct ToolUsage` with a declaration link to this requirement. `documented_exports` walks the rustdoc HTML generated for the current source tree. It fails if rustdoc exports a struct that is not in the inventory, or if the inventory lists one that rustdoc lacks. It then calls `check_declaration` for `trust_but_verify::graph::ToolUsage`.\n- **Struct declaration:** `check_declaration` extracts the first `rust,ignore` block from the requirement text and compares it with the declaration rustdoc publishes, using `signature_parts`. The comparison is two-way (missing and unexpected). It would catch changed field names, field types (`u64`) or visibility, an added or removed field, and a missing `#[non_exhaustive]`. It also asserts that a struct with public fields is `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- **Trait implementations:** `check_declaration` parses the second code block into `impl Clone/Copy/Debug/Default/Serialize for ToolUsage`. It asserts set equality with `rustdoc.trait_impls(page)`, which reads rustdoc's trait-implementations section. A missing derive, such as dropping `Copy`, `Default` or `Serialize`, fails the test. An extra public trait impl also fails it. This matches the exact list the requirement gives.\n- **Members:** it asserts that the indexed member set matches the inventory.\n\nThe requirement states no serialized form and no `Default` value semantics, so existence of the impls is the whole obligation. I found no unacknowledged gap. The test is marked with the requirement, so there is no marking gap.",
  "references": "0d76678f3ab137bf77b6a76d0cd17c460f60426827a412971b8cd589c65e25da",
